window.__finlexDocLoaded(10206,{"id":10206,"articles":[{"id":1000859,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Covers historical-cost accounting for PP&E, depreciation, the two-step undiscounted-cash-flow impairment test for long-lived assets held and used (ASC 360-10-35), and held-for-sale classification.","seq":0},{"id":1000860,"no":"ASC 360-10-35","heading":"Historical Cost Model & Two-Step Undiscounted Impairment Test","text":"• Strict Historical Cost Only: PP&E is carried at cost less accumulated depreciation and impairment — upward revaluation is strictly prohibited.\n• Asset Group Level (ASC 360-10-35-23): Test long-lived assets held and used at the lowest level for which identifiable cash flows are largely independent of other asset groups.\n• Two-Step Impairment Test (ASC 360-10-35-17~21) — Triggered ONLY by Impairment Indicators:\n  – Step 1 (Recoverability Screen): Compare carrying amount of the asset group to the sum of UNDISCOUNTED future net cash flows over the remaining useful life of the primary asset. If undiscounted cash flows >= carrying amount, NO impairment is recorded (even if Fair Value < carrying amount!).\n  – Step 2 (Measurement): Only if Step 1 fails, recognize impairment equal to carrying amount minus FAIR VALUE (ASC 820). Allocate loss pro rata across long-lived assets (not reducing any asset below its individual determinable fair value).\n• Prohibition of Reversals (35-20): Impairment losses on held-and-used assets establish a new cost basis and can NEVER be reversed.","seq":1},{"id":1000861,"no":"ASC 360-10-45","heading":"Long-Lived Assets Held for Sale & Component Depreciation","text":"• Six Held-for-Sale Criteria (ASC 360-10-45-9): Management committed to plan to sell; available for immediate sale; active program to locate buyer; sale probable within 1 year; actively marketed at reasonable price; unlikely plan will change significantly.\n• Measurement (45-9 & 35-43): Cease depreciation; measure at lower of carrying amount or Fair Value Less Cost to Sell. Subsequent fair value recoveries ARE recognized up to cumulative prior write-downs while held for sale.\n• Component Depreciation: Permitted under US GAAP but NOT mandatory (unlike IAS 16.43).","seq":2},{"id":1000862,"no":"ASC 360 vs IAS 16 / IAS 36 / CAS 4 & 8","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Divergences (2-Step Undiscounted vs. 1-Step Discounted)","text":"• Impairment Trigger Frequency: ASC 360's Step 1 uses UNDISCOUNTED cash flows, making impairments significantly less frequent than IAS 36 / CAS 8's 1-step discounted Recoverable Amount test.\n• Reversals & Revaluation: Both upward revaluation (IAS 16) and non-goodwill impairment reversal (IAS 36) are permitted under IFRS, whereas US GAAP strictly prohibits both (China CAS 4/8 also prohibit both).","seq":3},{"id":1000863,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":4}]});
